# Local settings for the HPC scripts. Copy to hpc/local.conf, which is
# gitignored, and edit. Nothing here is committed: an account name, a login node
# and a path on your own disk are not the repository's business.
#
#   cp hpc/local.conf.example hpc/local.conf
#
# Every setting can also be given as an environment variable, which takes
# precedence over this file. The names follow bayesnec's own
# hpc/local.conf.example and the example7 compendium's, so one set of habits
# covers all three.

# The cluster, as ssh would take it. Required.
HOST=you@login-node.example.org

# Where the job directory goes on the cluster. Defaults to
# /export/scratch/<the account in HOST>/grouping-structures.
#DEST=/export/scratch/you/grouping-structures

# The container image on this machine, needed only to copy it to the cluster the
# first time. This study runs inside bayesnec's precompile image, which holds the
# toolchain and not bayesnec; the job installs bayesnec from the source this
# repository pins. Build it from the bayesnec repository:
#
#   cd ../bayesnec && ./hpc/build.sh
#
#SIF=/home/you/tmp/bayesnec-precompile.sif

# A checkout of open-AIMS/bayesnec on this machine, and the ref within it that
# carries example8. The vignette is still on a branch, so the ref is a branch
# name and deploy.sh resolves it to a commit and records that in
# hpc/bayesnec.lock. Once it has merged, pin a tag or a commit here instead.
#BAYESNEC_REPO=../bayesnec
#BAYESNEC_REF=issue-6-33-grouping-vignette

# How many array tasks may be resident at once. The study is embarrassingly
# parallel, so throughput scales with this up to the width of the array.
#MAX_RESIDENT=60
